# Design: Shadow baselines and host integration (T038)

## Problem
MVP_SPEC.md sections 5 and 9-10 require one shadow run to record the
new outcome beside the existing decision, without touching the
existing decision path. The report contract held a baseline field that
no wrapper could state, and nothing pinned the host integration
boundary: the latency of the awaited call, nonblocking work, and the
application actions that stay outside the library.

## Approach
The host states its decision through the baseline option of run
({ outcome, revision }). The Rust core validates it at run creation
through parse_baseline, the same parser that owns the stored report
contract, and every terminal report records it beside the aggregate.
Baseline::validate_for_mode owns the mode rule and the bounds for the
builder and the parse path alike, so both wrappers get one authority.

## Key Trade-offs
- A baseline is refused in enforcement mode instead of ignored. One
  field that means "the decision that already happened" has no meaning
  in one run that exists to produce the decision, and silence would
  let one stored report blur the two modes.
- The library computes no agreement. The baseline keeps the host's own
  decision vocabulary (one free bounded string), because only the host
  knows what its decision words mean, so any agreement classification
  the library invented would be an accuracy claim the report cannot
  support. The host compares the two recorded values itself.
- Baseline validation sits in RunState::new, not only in report
  assembly. Assembly runs at the terminal event, so a bounds check
  there would fail one run after it spent its attempts.
- Latency is documented as observable and bounded, not summed into one
  new totals field: per-check timing plus the total deadline already
  state what one awaited call adds, and filling totals is run-report
  work outside this task.
- Nonblocking work stays a host pattern. The library arms no timer
  that outlives one resolved call and exports no queue or scheduler
  operation, so one host-owned durable queue worker owns the complete
  lifecycle. A detached library job would hide the boundary that
  MVP_SPEC.md section 10 draws.

